            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Oh it does, that's craaazy.\r\n\r\nHaving thought about this some more, I think the behavior I would expect (only thinking about my usecase with argument lists, I'm sure there are others this wouldn't make senes for) is something like this:\r\n\r\n - putting the cursor on the comma would always select the argument to the left, the comma itself, and whitespace to the right of the comma (if there is whitespace)\r\n - putting the cursor on the whitespace would select the argument to the right of it, the comma following that argument, and the space following that comma (so not the whitespace where the cursor is initially)\r\n - special case: if we're on the last argument in the argument list, we select backwards to include previous space\r\n\r\nThat's maybe a bit weird because in the case of putting the cursor on the whitespace it would select a text object that the cursor is not really on, but I think this matches the behavior people expect, and I think this would both delete the \"correct\" argument in all cases as well maintaining the correct spacing, both for normal cases like I showed `(foo, bar, qux)` and more padded ones like `( foo, bar, qux )` and also handle trailing spaces in both cases `(foo, bar, qux,)` and `( foo, bar, qux, )`. \r\n\r\nThe special case I think is unavoidable because argument lists don't usually have trailing commas, and I think maybe special casing on the last argument gives the behavior you would most expect? ",
