            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I don't necessarily see why that wouldn't work. I have constructed some examples below to illustrate the different cases. Star `*` means cursor position, underscores `_` are used to mark selection/deletion.\r\n\r\n```\r\n(aaa, bbb, ccc)\r\n(aaa* bbb, ccc) -> (_____bbb, ccc)\r\n(aaa,*bbb, ccc) -> (aaa, _____ccc)\r\n(aaa, bbb* ccc) -> (aaa, _____ccc)\r\n(aaa, bbb,*ccc) -> (aaa, bbb_____)\r\n\r\n( aaa, bbb, ccc )\r\n( aaa* bbb, ccc ) -> ( _____bbb, ccc )\r\n( aaa,*bbb, ccc ) -> ( aaa, _____ccc )\r\n( aaa, bbb* ccc ) -> ( aaa, _____ccc )\r\n( aaa, bbb,*ccc ) -> ( aaa, bbb_____ )\r\n\r\n(aaa, bbb, ccc,)\r\n(aaa* bbb, ccc,) -> (_____bbb, ccc,)\r\n(aaa,*bbb, ccc,) -> (aaa, _____ccc,)\r\n(aaa, bbb* ccc,) -> (aaa, _____ccc,)\r\n(aaa, bbb,*ccc,) -> (aaa, bbb,_____) // no trailing space, therefore we expand back\r\n\r\n( aaa, bbb, ccc, )\r\n( aaa* bbb, ccc, ) -> ( _____bbb, ccc, )\r\n( aaa,*bbb, ccc, ) -> ( aaa, _____ccc, )\r\n( aaa, bbb* ccc, ) -> ( aaa, _____ccc, )\r\n( aaa, bbb,*ccc, ) -> ( aaa, bbb, _____)\r\n\r\n(aaa , bbb , ccc ,)\r\n(aaa*, bbb , ccc ,) -> (______bbb , ccc ,)\r\n(aaa ,*bbb , ccc ,) -> (aaa , ______ccc ,)\r\n(aaa , bbb*, ccc ,) -> (aaa , ______ccc ,)\r\n(aaa , bbb ,*ccc ,) -> (aaa , bbb ,______) // no trailing space, same rule as above\r\n\r\n( aaa , bbb , ccc , )\r\n( aaa*, bbb , ccc , ) -> ( ______bbb , ccc , )\r\n( aaa ,*bbb , ccc , ) -> ( aaa , ______ccc , )\r\n( aaa , bbb*, ccc , ) -> ( aaa , ______ccc , )\r\n( aaa , bbb ,*ccc , ) -> ( aaa , bbb , ______)\r\n( aaa , bbb , ccc ,*) -> ( aaa , bbb , ccc ,?) // I don't know what this would mean, no-op?\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith this additional rule about potentially grabbing whitespace backwards if there aren't any trailing spaces, I think it will behave as expected in every usecase.\r\n\r\nHowever, of course, how it's implemented and the constraints of the existing solution I have no idea, just a suggestion for a better user experience! ",
