            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I don't necessarily see why that wouldn't work.\r\n\r\nWith the rule \"putting the cursor on the whitespace would select the argument to the right of it, the comma following that argument, and the space following that comma (so not the whitespace where the cursor is initially)\" the following cases will behave not as you described: `(aaa*, bbb , ccc ,)` and `( aaa , bbb*, ccc , )`.\r\n\r\nBut yes, it is not quite as simple as deciding what to do based on the cursor. It is more about the overall rule that should detect *any* argument after which built-in algorithm chooses the \"best\" one (taking into account search method, etc.). I am fairly certain that the current behavior of argument textobject won't change (especially considering that there is some customization options in `gen_spec.argument()`).",
            "created_at": "2024-10-31T16:21:46Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1317#issuecomment-2450295420",
            "id": 2450295420,
