        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> the following cases will behave not as you described: \r\n\r\nFair enough, my rule should have been more precise. I was thinking of the space following a comma, so really it should have been:\r\n\"putting the cursor on whitespace following a comma would select the argument to the right of it, the comma following that argument, and any space following that comma\".\r\n\r\nDo you not think this rule would give the correct result in all cases? If you look at my examples I think it does.\r\n\r\nI understand that you don't really want to change it, but the way it currently behaves it's honestly kind of broken. I understand that you also want to consider all edge cases, but as it stands it messes up the formatting for completely standard lists like `(aaa, bbb, ccc)`. Isn't that worse than potentially having weird behavior for weirdly formatted lists? It doesn't get any more run-of-the-mill, regular argument list than that. And with the customization you suggested (or my variant) it deletes the wrong arguments if you happen to put the cursor on a comma or whitespace (in some but not all cases, making it even worse!). \r\n\r\nWouldn't it be better if it selected the correct arguments even if you have your cursor on a comma, _and_ didn't mess up the formatting, both in regular cases and in edge cases? ",
            "created_at": "2024-10-31T16:38:21Z",
